// Garrison control plane — the console login, extended.
//
// SchemaForge seeds a system `User` schema as the login store for this
// administrative console. This file redeclares it with two additional columns
// so a console login can carry directory identity into its token:
//
//   entra_object_id  the Entra ID objectId of the person behind the login
//   org_slug         the Organization the login belongs to
//
// `[schema_forge.authz.principal_claims]` in config.toml reads both columns
// off the row at every login and refresh and projects them onto
// `Forge::Principal`, which is what lets policies/custom/directory-identity.cedar
// require a directory identity for an audit export.
//
// The first eight fields are the seeded definition verbatim; `apply` diffs
// this against the stored metadata and lands only the additions. Dropping the
// schema is refused by SchemaForge's system-schema protection.
//
// `directory_service` is the reconciler that flips `active` when the
// directory disables or removes a person. The `@field_access` fences on
// `roles`, `role_rank`, and `metadata` are what stop that bearer from
// promoting a login through the entity route; `/api/v1/forge/users` keeps its
// own role-rank guards on top. A console user holding `platform_admin` is
// never deactivated by the sync; that rule lives in the reconciler, not here,
// because the DSL cannot express it.

@system
@display("email")
@access(read: ["directory_service"], write: ["directory_service"])
schema User {
    email:           text(max: 512) required indexed
    display_name:    text(max: 255) required
    roles:           text[] @field_access(write: ["platform_admin"])
    role_rank:       integer required @field_access(write: ["platform_admin"])
    active:          boolean default(true)
    password_hash:   text(max: 512) @hidden
    last_login:      datetime
    metadata:        json @field_access(write: ["platform_admin"])

    entra_object_id: text(max: 64) unique indexed
    org_slug:        text(max: 100) indexed
}
